  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 103 PM EST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nA large eastern CONUS low pressure system, double-barreled with one \ncenter lifting up the east coast and another taking a northeast \ntrack just west of the Appalachians, will continue to provide the \nlocal area with blustery/cool conditions and a steady rain for the \nremainder of today through at least Friday morning. Aggressive \nmoisture surge and isentropic ascent within a 50-65 kt easterly jet \nin the 850-700 mb layer brings more moderate rainfall this afternoon \nand evening. Deformation forcing lingering on the western flank of \nthe system by later tonight and Friday with rainfall intensity and \ncoverage likely waning a bit with time, especially by later Friday \nmorning and afternoon when some dry pockets emerge. Modest CAA on \nthe backside of the low cold make it possible for wet snow to mix in \nlater tonight into Friday morning in spots. Still not expecting much \nin the way of impacts given the wet ground and sfc temps/dewpoints \nholding in the mid-upper 30s. The primary impacts from this system \nstill appear to be the widespread 1\" or so rainfall totals, and \ngusty northeast winds this afternoon-evening in the 30-40 mph range. \n\nSaturday will feature weak warm advection and some sunshine as the \nlarge low pressure system exits east and a trailing small scale \nperturbation bypasses north of the area. A deeper, more progressive \nshortwave trough remains on target to track east through the OH \nValley and Great Lakes Sunday into Sunday evening. Ensemble and \ndeterministic model solutions now generally agree on an associated \nsfc low track through the OH Valley. Forcing and moisture return \nwill bring another round of primarily rain with amounts up to near a \nhalf inch possible. Much of next week then appears mainly dry with \ngradual moderation thanks to a transition to a lower amplitude, \nsplit flow regime.",
